      "id": 1,
      "text": "Walnut Grove was first settled in the early 1810s and originally called Thomas Cross Roads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/walnut-grove/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1848, the town was named Cornelius after postmaster Harvey Cornelius, then renamed Walnut Grove a year later.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walnut_Grove%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the Civil War, Walnut Grove was burned, leaving a scar in its history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walnut_Grove%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town once hosted Walnut Grove College, a preparatory school that operated for 15 years and boasted 13 stores, including a soda fountain drug store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
